<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amatitlán</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amatitlán is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> on the shore of Lake Amatitlán, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. In the nineteenth century its surroundings, together with those of Antigua, were the main centre of cochineal production, the red dye that was Guatemala's chief export until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">synthetic dyes</a> ruined the trade from the 1860s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amatitlan.jpg" alt="Amatitlán" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Montaje realizado por Merari Rene 0901. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amatitl%C3%A1n_-_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Carrera, exports of cochineal dye from the valleys around Antigua and <b>Amatitlán</b> sustained the economy until synthetic dyes ruined the trade in the 1850s and 1860s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
